Q1. Deadlock Debugging (10 Marks)
1. Identify the Problem
  A deadlock occurs between Process P1 and Process P2.
Both processes wait indefinitely for a resource held by the other process.
As a result, neither process can enter the critical section.

2. Root Cause
  P1 locks R1 and waits for R2.
  P2 locks R2 and waits for R1.
Both processes hold one resource while waiting for another.
This creates a circular wait, causing deadlock.

3. Deadlock Conditions
  Mutual Exclusion: Only one process can access a resource at a time.
  Hold and Wait: Processes hold one resource while requesting another.
  No Preemption: Resources cannot be forcibly taken away.
  Circular Wait: P1 waits for P2, and P2 waits for P1.

4. Optimization
Acquire resources in the same order (R1 → R2) for all processes.
Use resource ordering to eliminate circular wait.
Implement timeout or deadlock detection and recovery if required.

5. Result
Deadlock is prevented.
Processes execute without blocking each other.
Resource utilization and system performance improve.

Q2. CPU Utilization Problem (10 Marks)
1. Identify the Problem
CPU utilization is only 30%.
The CPU remains idle even when processes are available.

2. Root Cause
Processes repeatedly move between Ready and Waiting states.
Processes are mostly I/O-bound.
CPU waits while processes perform I/O operations.

3. Explanation
Frequent I/O requests reduce CPU execution time.
The scheduler cannot keep the CPU busy.
This lowers overall system throughput.

4. Optimization
Use asynchronous I/O to reduce blocking.
Schedule CPU-bound processes while I/O-bound processes wait.
Improve buffering and caching techniques.
Optimize the scheduling algorithm.

5. Result
CPU idle time decreases.
CPU utilization increases.
Throughput and system efficiency improve.

Q3. Memory Bottleneck Debugging (10 Marks)
1. Identify the Problem
The program experiences frequent page faults.
Performance becomes slow due to excessive memory access.

2. Root Cause
Large heap size (150 MB).
25 threads consume additional stack memory.
Poor memory access causes frequent page loading from disk.

3. Explanation
Page faults force the operating system to access disk memory.
Disk access is much slower than RAM access.
Frequent page faults increase execution time.

4. Optimizations
Reduce the number of threads using a thread pool.
Optimize heap memory allocation.
Free unused memory whenever possible.
Improve memory locality to reduce paging.

5. Result
Page faults decrease.
Memory utilization improves.
Application performance becomes faster.

Q4. Starvation Debugging (10 Marks)
1. Identify the Problem
Process P5 never receives CPU time.
It remains in the ready queue for a long period.

2. Root Cause
P5 belongs to the Q3 (Batch Queue).
Q1 and Q2 always have higher-priority processes.
Continuous arrival of high-priority processes delays P5 indefinitely.

3. Explanation
The scheduler always selects higher-priority queues first.
Lower-priority processes are postponed repeatedly.
This leads to starvation.

4. Optimization
Apply Aging to gradually increase the priority of waiting processes.
Reserve CPU time for lower-priority queues.
Use fair scheduling techniques.

5. Result
Starvation is eliminated.
Fair CPU allocation is achieved.
Every process eventually gets CPU time.

Q5. Context-Switch Overhead Debugging (10 Marks)
1. Identify the Problem
The server performs 20,000 context switches per second.
Excessive context switching reduces system performance.

2. Root Cause
Very small time quantum (1 ms).
Average CPU burst time is 2 ms.
Frequent I/O operations cause repeated process switching.

3. Explanation
Each context switch saves and restores process states.
Too many context switches waste CPU time.
Useful CPU execution time is reduced.

4. Optimizations
Increase the time quantum (e.g., 4–5 ms).
Reduce I/O blocking using asynchronous I/O.
Batch I/O requests where possible.
Tune the scheduler to reduce unnecessary preemption.

5. Result
Context switches decrease.
CPU overhead is reduced.
Throughput and overall system performance improve.
